Verdict: MINOR  
Retrieval adequacy: adequate

Strengths
- The redesign is now anchored to the disclosure-layer boundary from `#334`, not the legacy sanction dataset.
- The files-to-change and regression boundaries are mostly consistent with the stated scope split between disclosure contracts and sanction surfaces.

Findings by severity

critical
- None.

high
- The plan still leaves the conflict-result contract underdefined. It says source priority must “affect conflict reasoning deterministically,” but the deliverable, pseudocode, tests, and acceptance criteria do not lock down the shape of that reasoning output. Without specifying whether the contract returns a code, enum, structured payload, or prioritized winner reference, different implementations can all satisfy the current text while producing incompatible downstream behavior.

medium
- `test_parent_disclosure_surface_remains_compatible` is weakly specified because the child issue explicitly must not “re-open or replace” the parent disclosure schema. As written, this test can collapse into a vague import/instantiation check and may not prove the contract actually composes with the `#334` schema boundary in a meaningful way.
- The duplicate/conflict rule uses “same value/citation payload,” but the payload boundary is not defined. If payload includes all citation fields plus confidence plus optional locator variants, implementations may diverge on whether small provenance differences are duplicates, conflicts, or invalid rows.
- The plan says `page_reference` should be treated as a general locator field, but the required field name remains `page_reference`. That is workable, but it creates a naming/semantics mismatch that will likely leak into validator and test wording unless explicitly normalized.

low
- `source_url` says “absolute http/https” in pseudocode, but acceptance criteria do not repeat the scheme restriction.
- The plan does not say whether duplicate/conflict classification is only against `existing_rows` or also within the incoming batch. That is likely to matter once ingestion is implemented.

Missing tests
- A positive-path test for a fully valid disclosure row with all citation fields populated and accepted.
- A test fixing the exact conflict-reason payload shape or code set.
- A test fixing whether source priority annotates conflicts only or also identifies a preferred source class within the result.
- A test for duplicate/conflict behavior among rows within the same ingest batch, not just versus `existing_rows`.
- A test proving non-PDF locators are accepted in `page_reference` when used as section/table identifiers.

Scope creep concerns
- `__init__.py` export expansion is still acceptable, but only if it stays limited to disclosure-contract primitives; exporting broader ingestion helpers would start widening the package boundary.
- Any attempt to make the child test suite assert parent schema details beyond compatibility will drift back into `#334` ownership.

Weakest assumption
- That “source-priority affects conflict reasoning” can remain partially specified at plan time without causing incompatible implementations later.

Most likely implementation failure mode
- The implementer ships a contract that classifies rows correctly into `accepted/duplicate/conflict/invalid`, but encodes conflict reasoning in an ad hoc structure that later automation cannot rely on consistently.

Most likely test gap
- Tests will prove bucket membership but not the semantics of the returned reason payload, so incompatible conflict annotations can still pass.

Future issues suggested
- A narrow follow-up to define conflict adjudication semantics separately from ingestion classification if future automation needs winner-selection, override rules, or review workflows.
- A locator-normalization follow-up if web-native disclosures require richer structured references than a single `page_reference` string.

Review confidence
- High
